ding her summers  in mexico visiting her dad's family  there  everyone from her grandparents to her  young cousins practice the sport i would  see them and i would be like wow i wish  i could do that back home because if i  want to do that i have to come to mexico  but what really drove me was when i had  a cousin of mine tell me you're never  going to be in escaramusa because you're  gringa  the sport was only introduced  competitively in the u.s in 1991 so for  paola there were few teams to perform  with and for me it was kind of that  moment that it clicked  like i have all odds stacked against me  gereria was a tradition paola's father  juan mario left behind when he was a  young man and crossed the border to work  and eventually start a family in the u.s  what was it like for you when paola said  she wanted to continue the family's  tradition here i was really happy  inside  that she want to follow their dreams and  i was really happy that she chose a  charity  so happy he bought then 11 year old  paola her own horse barbie and found her  a team to compete with in buckley  washington just about 2 400 miles away  from mexico  for the next decade paola and barbie  would train almost daily at the family's  ranch and travel throughout the u.s and  mexico competing and winning  when we practice we wake up sometimes at  four in the morning you'll have boot  camp sessions so you'll do three days of  writing two to three times a day your  objective is perfection and to win and  that takes so much work and so much  sacrifice  the sport takes endurance discipline  and deep pockets the rules are strict  especially when it comes to the team's  attire we actually have to ride in  earrings that are handmade so they have  to be artisan made in mexico oh my god  the rules are unbelievable yeah there's  so many rules yeah from the silver  threads to the sombrero to the patriotic  embroidery all tells a story about  mexico's history and heritage  last month paula triumphantly re